NAME
       smbclient - list and read Windows file shares (Cyber Range)

SYNOPSIS
       smbclient -L //SERVER [-N] [-U USER[%PASSWORD]]
       smbclient //SERVER/SHARE [-N] [-U USER[%PASSWORD]] [-c "COMMANDS"]

DESCRIPTION
       smbclient talks to the file sharing service (SMB, TCP port 445) of a
       Windows computer. With -L it lists the shares; with //SERVER/SHARE it
       opens one and shows an smb: \> prompt, much like an ftp client.

       In Cyber Range smbclient reaches only the range's pretend Windows hosts.
       Their shares are read-only for you.

OPTIONS
       -L //SERVER
              List the server's shares (ADMIN$, C$ and IPC$ are always there).

       -N     No password: sign in as an anonymous visitor (a "null session").
              Shares that allow it open; the others say ACCESS_DENIED.

       -U USER[%PASSWORD]
              Sign in as an account. Without %PASSWORD smbclient asks for it.
              A domain account may be written DOMAIN\\USER.

       -c "COMMANDS"
              Run the commands (separated by ;) and leave.

COMMANDS AT THE smb: \> PROMPT
       ls [MASK]           list the folder (files marked H are hidden)
       cd FOLDER           change folder (.. goes up)
       pwd                 show where you are
       get FILE [LOCAL]    copy a file to the current folder on your box
       more FILE           show a file
       exit                leave

EXAMPLES
       Which shares does the server offer to anyone?

           smbclient -L //10.10.1.30 -N

       Copy a file from the Public share:

           smbclient //10.10.1.30/Public -N -c 'ls; get readme.txt'

       Sign in as an account:

           smbclient //10.10.1.30/Finance -U kim

NOTES
       Only test computers you own or have written permission to test.
       Everything in Cyber Range is pretend: no real share is touched.

SEE ALSO
       winrm-shell(1), nmap(1), ftp(1)
